  <div class="neo-card neo-panel neo-reading" style="max-width:850px;margin:0 auto 65px">
   <p>AI assistants are undergoing a change; they can now not only answer questions but also carry out tasks, use applications, and continue working in the background.</p>
   <p>Grok Bot, Meta Muse, and OpenAI Dots are three interesting products. The following is a comparison of them.</p>
   <h2>What are they?</h2>
   <h3>Grok Bot — xAI</h3>
   <p>Grok Bot functions more in the way of a digital teammate—you can give it tasks and it is able to use tools and applications in order to carry them out.</p>
   <p>This is especially interesting for developers, researchers, and those who need assistance with technical work.</p>
   <h3>Meta Muse — Meta</h3>
   <p>Muse pays more attention to everyday life and can assist with planning, making bookings, sending messages and other personal matters.</p>
   <p>It operates within its own cloud environment and is able to link with the services that you currently use.</p>
   <h3>OpenAI Dots — OpenAI</h3>
   <p>Dots are suitable for use when work is going on continuously; they remember useful context, link with various apps, and can make progress on tasks without requiring the need for repeated instructions.</p>
   <p>For instance, a Dot could keep an eye on a project, see research updates, or assist with preparing reports over time.</p>
   <h2>Quick comparison</h2>
   <div style="overflow-x:auto"><table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;text-align:left">
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Feature</th><th scope="col">Grok Bot</th><th scope="col">Meta Muse</th><th scope="col">OpenAI Dots</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
     <tr><th scope="row">Company</th><td>xAI</td><td>Meta</td><td>OpenAI</td></tr>
     <tr><th scope="row">Main focus</th><td>Technical work</td><td>Personal tasks</td><td>Ongoing work</td></tr>
     <tr><th scope="row">Background tasks</th><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
     <tr><th scope="row">App integrations</th><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
     <tr><th scope="row">Cloud computer</th><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
     <tr><th scope="row">Best for</th><td>Developers</td><td>Individuals</td><td>Professionals and teams</td></tr>
    </tbody>
   </table></div>
   <h2>Then I should pick which one?</h2>
   <p>The aspect that interests me more is Grok Bot and OpenAI Dots as a developer.</p>
   <p>Grok Bot appears to be useful for carrying out technical tasks, whereas Dots seems more appropriate for work that lasts over several days or involves use of multiple applications.</p>
   <p>Muse is geared towards a different area. Although it might prove useful for ordinary tasks, it isn't the one I would choose when it comes to software development.</p>
   <p>There's one aspect that I'd like to investigate namely just how reliably these agents carry out tasks without any human involvement. A good demonstration is one thing but achieving consistent results on actual projects is something else.</p>
   <h2>What about pricing?</h2>
   <p>Each of them has a different access model.</p>
   <ul>
    <li><strong>Grok Bot:</strong> It is available via eligible subscriptions.</li>
    <li><strong>Meta Muse:</strong> Meta Muse offers free access in some cases, along with paid options.</li>
    <li><strong>OpenAI Dots:</strong> OpenAI Dots are available as part of eligible ChatGPT plans.</li>
   </ul>
   <p>The specific prices and usage limits will vary according to the plan and the way it is being introduced.</p>
   <h2>Final thoughts</h2>
   <p>At the moment I don't consider there to be a definite winner.</p>
   <p>Grok Bot seems promising when it comes to technical tasks, Muse is suitable for personal assistance, and Dots is appropriate for continuous professional work.</p>
   <p>What is important to me is this: can I give an agent a job, place my trust in it to make progress, and then check the result without having to keep monitoring it?</p>
   <p>What I'll do is keep an eye on how these products develop.</p>
